Subclinical Vitamin D Insufficiency
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Serum 25(OH)D specifically between 20-29 ng/mL | Normal serum calcium and intact PTH | Asymptomatic or vague generalized musculoskeletal ache

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-027
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of diffuse skeletal pain and proximal weakness leading to a near-fall, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude an acute pathological fracture or spinal cord compression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, severe weight-bearing difficulty and profound musculoskeletal pain. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an evolving compressive myelopathy, acute fracture, or polymyalgia rheumatica crisis, preventing irreversible structural damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My bones literally ache from the inside, and when I tried to stand up from the chair, my thighs just gave out completely.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a subtle, long-standing history of limited sun exposure and a strict indoor lifestyle, evolving into vague, generalized musculoskeletal aching. Over the last 48 hours, there has been a significant escalation of nonspecific lower back pain and subtle proximal muscle weakness, culminating in a near-fall today. The patient describes mild, persistent fatigue and a mildly depressed mood with seasonal affective shifts. They report an increased susceptibility to recurrent mild upper respiratory infections.
Functional Status: Currently resting in a chair; requires spousal assistance to mobilize safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate fall risk due to proximal thigh weakness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/82 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient appears fatigued and in diffuse, non-focal pain upon movement. A review of past medical history notes a recent hospital admission for a sudden focal deficit with complete resolution <24h and a normal diffusion-weighted MRI, confirming a past TIA, which is currently stable and unrelated to tonight's presentation.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals distinct bone discomfort upon firm sternal and tibial palpation. There is mild bilateral proximal muscle weakness (4/5 in quadriceps) but an absence of overt signs of rickets, osteomalacia, or focal neurological deficits in the lower extremities. Deep tendon reflexes are 2+ and symmetric. Spinal palpation reveals no focal midline tenderness or step-offs.
Environmental Observations: House is heavily shaded with thick curtains drawn; distinct lack of natural light.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient compliant with daily aspirin and statin following the recent TIA.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subclinical Vitamin D Insufficiency - Status: Exacerbated by extreme deconditioning and indoor isolation.
Differentials:

Pathological Spinal Fracture - Ruled out due to the absence of severe localized midline spinal tenderness, lack of radicular pain, and intact sphincter tone.

Compressive Myelopathy - Ruled out due to symmetric reflexes, negative Babinski sign, and preserved distal motor strength.

Polymyalgia Rheumatica - Ruled out clinically due to the prominence of objective bone tenderness (sternal/tibial) rather than isolated shoulder/hip girdle stiffness, though requires formal inflammatory markers.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Fall risk).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours evaluation due to a sudden failure to weight-bear safely combined with severe deep bone pain. Telephone triage appropriately identified the critical necessity to rule out a pathological fracture or an evolving compressive neurological lesion. A meticulous neurological and musculoskeletal examination confirmed the absence of acute spinal cord compromise, radiculopathy, or focal osseous destruction. By definitively localizing the pain to a diffuse metabolic skeletal hypersensitivity and ruling out acute surgical emergencies, the clinician safely avoided an unnecessary, highly traumatic emergency department transfer, managing the immediate fall risk in the home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral analgesia and initiated strict fall-prevention measures. The expected investigation results include serum 25-hydroxyvitamin D levels between 12 and 20 ng/mL with normal serum calcium and parathyroid hormone levels, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To rebuild proximal muscle strength and prevent falls, perform five sit-to-stand repetitions from a sturdy dining chair every morning before breakfast; engaging these major muscle groups daily will rapidly rebuild your thigh strength and directly improve your balance.
Home-Based Services: Referral placed for an urgent home physiotherapy assessment.
DME/Safety: Use a four-wheeled walker for all internal mobilization tonight.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a comprehensive metabolic panel, Vitamin D level, and bone density scan referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sudden loss of bladder or bowel control, new severe numbness in the groin area, or complete inability to move the legs.

